    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** leads the charge with an elite 82.87, buoyed by a favorable matchup against Tanner Bibee and a prime leadoff spot that maximizes his path to R and SB. Joining him at the top of our priority list is **Mickey Moniak** (75.98), whose value is significantly amplified by a massive 14% park boost and a strong platoon advantage against Michael Wacha. **Brice Turang** (75.06) remains a high-floor play despite a slight penalty for facing an elite-holding catcher; his speed profile and leadoff assignment against Ryan Johnson make him essential for our SB-starved roster. Meanwhile, **James Wood** (67.20) provides elite xwOBA stability, and **Josh Naylor** (66.60) continues to be a reliable engine in the heart of our order, serving as a pillar for RBI production.\n\nStrategic platoon edges are fueling several of our mid-tier starts today. **Zach McKinstry** (64.83) earns a spot thanks to a 6% boost from the platoon and a 19% bump from facing the vulnerable Jeffrey Springs. Conversely, our toughest personnel call involves **Isaac Paredes** (48.29). While he was the primary candidate to displace **Austin Riley** (48.57), the optimizer opted for **Austin Riley** to maintain roster stability, as **Isaac Paredes** faces a significantly tougher assignment in Nathan Eovaldi, whose 3.37 xERA effectively neutralized the platoon advantage. We are also monitoring the status of **Gabriel Moreno**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Cole Young**, and **Austin Riley** closely; as their lineups remain pending, any late-breaking shifts\u2014particularly regarding their expected batting order\u2014could impact these baseline projections. We remain vigilant regarding weather, though for now, the primary risks are tied to the defensive capabilities of opposing catchers curbing the stolen base upside for our fleet-footed threats like **Jose Caballero**.",
